"# kernel_llm: one round of the llm kernel\n\nThe body of the llm kernel's daemon (`kernel/llm-kernel`): the tool folder `$AOS_HOME/lib/aos/kernel_llm`, run by `aos-exec` (`aos-exec $AOS_HOME/lib/aos/kernel_llm [--enable-counters] kernel.json`); you do not run it by hand. Before 2026-11-14 this was a C++ program named `aos-llm-kernel`; settings, files and letters did not change, only the error prefix is now `kernel_llm:`. Start the kernel as its own project:\n\n```sh\ncp -r $AOS_HOME/kernel/llm-kernel llms/def\ncd llms && aos-exec --stack stacks/llm def      # then point agents at it: AOS_LLM_KERNEL=/abs/llms/stacks/llm\naos-daemon-ctl -d stacks/llm shutdown           # stop it\n```\n\nEach round it replies to finished brain calls and picks the best waiting letters from `$AOS_STACK/requests/` for the endpoint packs in `kernel.json` (slots, priority, budget). A bad letter gets an error reply; that is not the kernel's error. Run by hand without `AOS_STACK` = `StackMissing`. Agents only wait (they do not fail) while the kernel is down.\n\nWhere the brain calls run: each one is an add letter to `$AOS_KERNEL` when it is set, else to the ctl mailbox of `$AOS_DAEMON` (the kernel has no `next`). So `unset AOS_KERNEL` in the shell that starts the llm daemon unless you mean it. Hooks: `kernel.json` may have `hooks` (`before_verdict`, `after_verdict`, `before_reply`), written as for a kernel station (aos/kernel.json). The daemon.json of the template uses `$AOS_HOME`, so `AOS_HOME` must be set when you start it.\n"
